
EXPERIMENT NO. 7

TITLE: To Automate Application Deployment Using GitHub/Jenkins.

STEP 1: Create the Project Folder

Open CMD and execute:

mkdir ASDD-Experiment7
cd ASDD-Experiment7
git init


STEP 2: Create the Web Application

Run:

notepad index.html

Paste the following code and save:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>ASDD Experiment 7</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Automated Application Deployment</h1>
    <p>Application deployed using GitHub Actions.</p>
</body>
</html>


STEP 3: Create the GitHub Actions Workflow

Run:

mkdir .github
mkdir .github\workflows
notepad .github\workflows\deploy.yml

Paste the following YAML code and save:

name: Application Deployment

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Verify application
        run: test -f index.html

      - name: Deployment successful
        run: echo "Application deployment pipeline completed successfully!"


STEP 4: Commit the Files

Run:

git add .
git commit -m "Add application and deployment workflow"
git branch -M main

If Git asks for your identity, configure it using your own details:

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

Then repeat the git add and git commit commands if the commit has not yet been created.


STEP 5: Create a GitHub Repository

1. Open https://github.com/
2. Create a new repository named ASDD-Experiment7.
3. Select Public or Private.
4. Do not initialize it with a README or other files.


STEP 6: Connect the Local Repository to GitHub

Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your actual GitHub username.

Run:

git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/ASDD-Experiment7.git

If origin already exists and has the wrong URL, use:

git remote set-url origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/ASDD-Experiment7.git

Verify the URL:

git remote -v


STEP 7: Push the Project to GitHub

Run:

git push -u origin main

Expected result: The application and workflow files are uploaded to GitHub.


STEP 8: Verify GitHub Actions

1. Open your GitHub repository.
2. Click the Actions tab.
3. Open the latest workflow run.
4. Verify that the workflow completes with a green checkmark.

Expected result: The automated workflow runs successfully whenever changes are pushed to the main branch.


STEP 9: Configure GitHub Pages

1. Open the repository on GitHub.
2. Click Settings.
3. Select Pages from the left sidebar.
4. Under Build and deployment, select:
   Source: Deploy from a branch
   Branch: main
   Folder: / (root)
5. Click Save.

Note: GitHub Pages must be enabled and configured separately from the workflow created in Step 3.


STEP 10: Open the Deployed Website

Wait a few minutes for GitHub Pages to publish the site.

Go to Settings > Pages and look for the published website URL.

It will usually follow this format:

https://YOUR_USERNAME.github.io/ASDD-Experiment7/

Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your GitHub username.

Open the published URL in your browser.

Expected output:

Automated Application Deployment

Application deployed using GitHub Actions.


STEP 11: Test Automatic Execution

Modify index.html and save it.

For example, change the paragraph to:

<p>Application updated successfully.</p>

Then run:

git add .
git commit -m "Update application"
git push

Verify that a new workflow run appears in GitHub Actions.

After GitHub Pages finishes rebuilding the site, refresh the website to see the updated content.
